 (manager-account): Pask does not think self-checkout is the issue. He believes shoplifting has grown since the Dunmere bus interchange opened beside the car park on 16 February. Door-counter footfall averaged 14,100 visits a week in Q1, 15,200 in Q2 and 15,900 in Q3. He cites recorded shoplifting incidents in the store log: 6 in Q1, 11 in Q2 and 15 in Q3, mostly razors, cosmetics and batteries. 'My team is logging more than they ever have. We need a guard on the door.' Asked whether staff theft could be involved, he said he trusts his team and pointed to the ethics-line case being closed.\n- Ethics-line case 26-0871 and asset protection follow-up (log): Received 21 Aug, anonymous caller: 'The night stocker at Dunmere [name redacted] keeps taking boxes out the back door after close. Saw it on the 12th and the 19th.' Follow-up by AP investigator R. Okafor, 2 Sep: Reviewed rear-door and dock CCTV for 12 and 19 August, 22:00–06:00. On both nights the named stocker made two trips through the rear door carrying flattened cardboard bales to the compactor, as listed on the closing checklist. No merchandise or sealed cartons seen leaving. Night supervisor confirms bale runs are assigned to this employee. Case closed: unsubstantiated.\n- Store 14 interim shrink report, Q1–Q3 FY2026 (financial-record): Shrink as % of department sales, Q1 / Q2 / Q3. Grocery 0.8 / 1.1 / 1.3. Fresh 1.6 / 2.0 / 2.4. Health & beauty 2.9 / 3.6 / 4.4. General merchandise 2.0 / 2.5 / 3.1. Store total 1.5 / 1.9 / 2.3. Each department's share of store sales stayed within one point across all three quarters: grocery 46%, fresh 24%, health & beauty 14%, general merchandise 16%. Footnote: Q1–Q3 figures are interim estimates extrapolated from rotating department cycle counts. The annual full physical count, carried out by the independent count contractor, is scheduled for 7 December.\n- Email from Bram Castellane, Regional Operations Manager, 6 Nov (manager-account): Re: December count, store 14. Given holiday staffing pressure, I propose we take store 14 out of the 7 December full physical count and roll forward the Q3 book figure for year-end. We already know where that store's number is heading, and the count costs about 140 staff hours plus contractor fees. The other 37 stores would count as planned. Please confirm by 20 November so the contractor can re-plan its schedule.\n\nBRIEF:\nStore 14's rising shrink is not yet shown to be a real rise in losses. The figures are interim estimates taken from partial cycle counts. They carry a margin of about ±0.6 points per quarter, and two-quarter rises like this one were confirmed by the year-end count only about half the time (13 of 27). Store 14's own full-count shrink has stayed at 1.5–1.8% for three years. The shoplifting log count is not reliable evidence of more theft: the incident app launched chain-wide on 1 April, and recorded incidents rose across the chain while chain shrink stayed flat. The tip about the night stocker was checked against CCTV and found to be bale runs, so there is nothing to support it. A change in sales mix does not explain the rise. Self-checkout, customer theft and a rise in estimate error all remain open. Neighbouring stores without self-checkout show no similar rise, which weakly hints at something local, but it does not settle the cause.\n\nWhat to do: keep store 14 in the 7 December full count, which is the one measurement that can settle this. Do not drop the store from the count. Do not hire a guard, pull the lanes or discipline anyone until the count is compared with the interim figures. Then plan on the real result. I'm fairly confident in this, though not about the underlying cause.\n","numLines":21,"startLine":1,"totalLines":21}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01ERn7FiZ2q7hPULcERAvH3z","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
